QUESTIONS = [
    # CH53 National Commission for SCs L1 (+24)
    {"text":"Under which Article of the Constitution is the National Commission for Scheduled Castes established?","options":["Article 338","Article 338A","Article 338B","Article 340"],"correct_answer":"Article 338","explanation":"The National Commission for Scheduled Castes (NCSC) is a constitutional body directly established by Article 338 of the Constitution.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Constitutional Amendment bifurcated the combined National Commission for SCs and STs into two separate bodies?","options":["65th Amendment Act, 1990","89th Amendment Act, 2003","102nd Amendment Act, 2018","42nd Amendment Act, 1976"],"correct_answer":"89th Amendment Act, 2003","explanation":"The 89th Constitutional Amendment Act of 2003 bifurcated the combined National Commission for SCs and STs into two separate bodies: NCSC (Article 338) and NCST (Article 338A).","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"In which year did the separate National Commission for SCs come into existence following the 89th Amendment?","options":["1990","2001","2004","2014"],"correct_answer":"2004","explanation":"The separate National Commission for Scheduled Castes came into existence in 2004 following the bifurcation enabled by the 89th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Before the 65th Constitutional Amendment, how were the safeguards for SCs and STs monitored?","options":["By a parliamentary committee","By a Special Officer designated as the Commissioner for SCs and STs","By the National Human Rights Commission","By the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment solely"],"correct_answer":"By a Special Officer designated as the Commissioner for SCs and STs","explanation":"Originally, Article 338 provided for the appointment of a single Special Officer for SCs and STs (known as the Commissioner for SCs and STs) to investigate all matters relating to their constitutional safeguards.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the composition of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes?","options":["A Chairperson, a Vice-Chairperson, and three other members","A Chairperson and four other members","A Chairperson, a Vice-Chairperson, and five other members","A Chairperson and two other members"],"correct_answer":"A Chairperson, a Vice-Chairperson, and three other members","explanation":"The NCSC consists of a Chairperson, a Vice-Chairperson, and three other members. They are appointed by the President by warrant under his hand and seal.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who appoints the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and members of the NCSC?","options":["The Parliament","The Prime Minister","The President of India","The Chief Justice of India"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"The President of India appoints the members of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes by warrant under his hand and seal.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who determines the conditions of service and tenure of office for the members of the NCSC?","options":["The Parliament via an Act","The Supreme Court","The President of India","The Commission itself"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Under Article 338(2), the conditions of service and tenure of office of the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and other Members of the NCSC are determined by the President by rule.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the standard tenure of office for the Chairperson and members of the NCSC, as per the rules notified by the President?","options":["3 years","4 years","5 years","6 years or up to 65 years of age"],"correct_answer":"3 years","explanation":"According to the National Commission for Scheduled Castes Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson and Members (Conditions of Service and Tenure) Rules, 2004, they hold office for a term of 3 years.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Is a member of the NCSC eligible for reappointment?","options":["No, they cannot be reappointed","Yes, for any number of terms","Yes, but for not more than two terms","Yes, but only if they are elevated to Chairperson"],"correct_answer":"Yes, but for not more than two terms","explanation":"The rules explicitly state that the Chairperson, Vice-Chairperson, and other Members of the Commission are not eligible for appointment for more than two terms.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following bodies is a constitutional body?","options":["National Commission for Women","National Commission for Scheduled Castes","National Human Rights Commission","National Commission for Minorities"],"correct_answer":"National Commission for Scheduled Castes","explanation":"The NCSC is directly established by Article 338 of the Constitution. The others mentioned are statutory bodies established by Acts of Parliament.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes?","options":["To investigate all matters relating to the constitutional safeguards for SCs","To inquire into specific complaints regarding the deprivation of rights of SCs","To convict individuals guilty of atrocities against SCs","To advise on the socio-economic development of SCs"],"correct_answer":"To convict individuals guilty of atrocities against SCs","explanation":"The NCSC is an investigatory and advisory body with powers of a civil court, but it is NOT a criminal court. It cannot convict or pass binding penal sentences against individuals.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"To whom does the NCSC present its annual report mapping the working of the safeguards for SCs?","options":["The Parliament directly","The President of India","The Ministry of Social Justice","The Supreme Court"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"The Commission presents an annual report containing its findings and recommendations to the President of India.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"What does the President do with the NCSC's report after receiving it?","options":["The President places it before the Union Cabinet only","The President forwards it to the Supreme Court for validation","The President causes it to be laid before each House of Parliament","The President archives it in the National Library"],"correct_answer":"The President causes it to be laid before each House of Parliament","explanation":"Under Article 338(6), the President shall cause all such reports to be laid before each House of Parliament along with a memorandum explaining the action taken on the recommendations.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"If an NCSC report contains recommendations relating to a specific State Government, what is the procedure?","options":["The NCSC sends it directly to the State Legislature","A copy of the report is forwarded to the Governor of the State, who places it before the State Legislature","The Union Home Minister summons the Chief Minister to discuss it","The President mandates the Governor to implement it via ordinance"],"correct_answer":"A copy of the report is forwarded to the Governor of the State, who places it before the State Legislature","explanation":"Under Article 338(7), if a report pertains to a state, a copy is forwarded to the Governor of the State, who causes it to be laid before the Legislature of the State along with an action-taken memorandum.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"While investigating any matter or inquiring into a complaint, the NCSC has all the powers of a:","options":["Supreme Court bench","High Court under criminal procedure","Civil court trying a suit","Special CBI Tribunal"],"correct_answer":"Civil court trying a suit","explanation":"Article 338(8) equips the Commission with the powers of a civil court trying a suit, allowing it to summon witnesses, require documents, and receive evidence on affidavits.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the NCSC summon anyone from any part of India?","options":["No, only individuals residing within the National Capital Territory","Yes, it can summon and enforce the attendance of any person from any part of India and examine them on oath","Yes, but only government officials","No, it requires prior permission from the local High Court"],"correct_answer":"Yes, it can summon and enforce the attendance of any person from any part of India and examine them on oath","explanation":"The constitutional vesting of civil court powers allows the Commission to summon and enforce the attendance of any person broadly from any part of India.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who must the Union and every State Government consult on all major policy matters affecting Scheduled Castes?","options":["The Election Commission","The NITI Aayog","The National Commission for Scheduled Castes","The National Commission for Backward Classes"],"correct_answer":"The National Commission for Scheduled Castes","explanation":"Article 338(9) mandates that the Union and every State Government shall consult the Commission on all major policy matters affecting Scheduled Castes.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Until 2018, which other community's constitutional safeguards did the NCSC also monitor, before a separate constitutional body was created for them?","options":["Scheduled Tribes","Other Backward Classes (OBCs)","Anglo-Indians","Religious Minorities"],"correct_answer":"Other Backward Classes (OBCs)","explanation":"Until the 102nd Amendment in 2018, the NCSC under Article 338(10) was also mandated to discharge similar functions with respect to the Other Backward Classes (OBCs).","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Currently, exactly which communities besides SCs fall under the investigatory purview of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes?","options":["The Anglo-Indian Community","The OBC community","The ST community","No other community"],"correct_answer":"The Anglo-Indian Community","explanation":"Under Article 338(10), the National Commission for SCs is also tasked with investigating matters concerning the Anglo-Indian community (the OBC mandate was transferred out in 2018).","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"The NCSC is headquartered in:","options":["Mumbai","Hyderabad","New Delhi","Nagpur"],"correct_answer":"New Delhi","explanation":"The headquarters of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes is situated in New Delhi. It also has regional and state offices.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the NCSC require the discovery and production of any document from a government department?","options":["No, government departments are immune","Yes, using its powers as a civil court","Yes, but only if the Prime Minister authorizes","Only if the matter relates to a criminal proceeding"],"correct_answer":"Yes, using its powers as a civil court","explanation":"Under Article 338(8)(b), the NCSC has the explicit civil court power to require the discovery and production of any document.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Are the recommendations of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes legally binding on the Government?","options":["Yes, they are absolute orders","No, they are recommendatory/advisory in nature","Yes, if endorsed by the Supreme Court","No, unless passed as a law by Parliament"],"correct_answer":"No, they are recommendatory/advisory in nature","explanation":"The NCSC, much like the NCST or Finance Commission, is an advisory body. Its recommendations are not legally binding decrees, though the government must explain its non-acceptance in Parliament.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the NCSC requisition any public record or copy thereof from any court or office?","options":["Yes, it is one of its civil court powers","No, court records are universally excluded","Only records from the subordinate judiciary","Only administrative (non-judicial) records"],"correct_answer":"Yes, it is one of its civil court powers","explanation":"Article 338(8)(d) specifically grants the Commission the power to requisition any public record or copy thereof from any court or office.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the status rank of the Chairperson of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes?","options":["Cabinet Minister of the Union","Minister of State of the Union","Secretary to the Government of India","Supreme Court Judge"],"correct_answer":"Cabinet Minister of the Union","explanation":"According to the warrant of precedence and service rules, the Chairperson of the NCSC has the rank of a Cabinet Minister of the Government of India.","chapter_number":53,"level":1},

    # CH53 National Commission for SCs L2 (+25)
    {"text":"Consider the evolution of the National Commission for SCs:\n1. Originally, the Constitution just provided for a 'Special Officer' under Article 338.\n2. In 1978, the Government resolved to set up an executive Multi-Member Commission alongside the Special Officer.\n3. The 65th Constitutional Amendment (1990) replaced both the Special Officer and the executive body with a high-level multi-member Constitutional body.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are historically and constitutionally correct. Before 1990, the statutory/executive bodies and the Special Officer coexisted with overlapping mandates, which the 65th Amendment resolved by creating a single unified Constitutional Commission for SCs & STs.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the status of the members of the NCSC:\n1. The Chairperson has the rank of a Union Cabinet Minister.\n2. The Vice-Chairperson has the rank of a Minister of State.\n3. The other members have the rank of a Secretary to the Government of India.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The NCSC rules establish these clear equivalencies in rank to ensure the Commission commands adequate authority within the bureaucratic and executive structure.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional powers of the NCSC while investigating complaints (Art 338(8)):\n1. It can issue commissions for the examination of witnesses and documents.\n2. It can sentence an accused person to minor imprisonment for contempt if they ignore a summons.\n3. It can receive evidence on affidavits.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. Although vested with civil court powers for issuing summons and taking evidence, the NCSC cannot pass penal sentences (like imprisonment) or function as a criminal contempt court. Its powers are for fact-finding and evidence gathering. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'consultation' clause in Article 338(9):\n1. The Union and State governments SHALL consult the Commission on all major policy matters affecting Scheduled Castes.\n2. According to the Supreme Court, this consultation must be 'meaningful' and not merely an empty formality.\n3. If a government fails to consult the NCSC, the policy is automatically void ab initio without judicial review.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Non-consultation does not automatically render a policy void without judicial review; it is an irregularity that courts take very seriously, but it isn't 'automatically void ab initio'. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect the constitutional mandate and judicial interpretation of 'meaningful' consultation.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Which of the following bodies/communities does the NCSC currently NOT hold the mandate to investigate safeguards for?\n1. Anglo-Indian Community\n2. Scheduled Tribes\n3. Other Backward Classes (OBCs)\nSelect the correct answer:","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Since 2003 (89th Amendment), STs are under the NCST. Since 2018 (102nd Amendment), OBCs are under the NCBC. The NCSC currently only oversees SCs and the Anglo-Indian community. Therefore, it does NOT hold the mandate for STs and OBCs.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the duties of the NCSC under Article 338(5):\n1. To inquire into specific complaints regarding the deprivation of rights and safeguards of the SCs.\n2. To participate and advise on the planning process of socio-economic development of SCs.\n3. To declare a specific community as a 'Scheduled Caste'.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The power to declare/notify a community as a Scheduled Caste lies with the President (via the initial order under Article 341) and any subsequent modifications can ONLY be made by Parliament by law. The NCSC cannot unilaterally declare an SC status.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the NCSC's relationship with the State Governments:\n1. If an NCSC report affects a state, the President routes it via the Union Home Ministry directly to the State's Chief Minister.\n2. The state Governor must lay the NCSC report before the state legislature, along with an explanatory memorandum of action taken.\n3. State Governments are constitutionally immune from being summoned by the NCSC.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 only","1 and 3 only","3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The report is forwarded to the *Governor* (usually via the nodal ministry), who places it before the legislature. Statement 3 is incorrect: Under its civil court powers (Art 338(8)), it can summon state officials and demand public records. Only 2 is correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the role of the NCSC in monitoring the 'Implementation of development programs':\n1. It evaluates the progress of the development of SCs under the Union and any State.\n2. It has the mandate to directly disburse developmental funds (like Post-Matric Scholarships) to SC students.\n3. It presents reports on the working of those safeguards annually or as it deems fit.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The NCSC is an evaluating, investigating, and advisory body. It does not execute development programs or disburse funds directly; those are executive functions carried out by ministries (like MSJE) and state departments. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding complaints of atrocities (like cases under the SC/ST Prevention of Atrocities Act):\n1. The NCSC can act as a parallel fast-track criminal court to expedite convictions.\n2. The NCSC can summon the investigating police officer to check the status of an FIR if a complaint of police inaction is received.\n3. The NCSC can order the state government to immediately transfer an accused civil servant.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 only","2 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; it is not a criminal court. Statement 3 is incorrect; it can *recommend* disciplinary action or transfer but cannot issue an executive 'order' forcing it. Statement 2 is correct; it routinely summons District Magistrates or SPs using its civil court powers to ensure proper investigation.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"The Supreme Court in *All India Indian Overseas Bank SC and ST Employees’ Welfare Association vs. Union of India (1996)* ruled on the powers of the NCSC (then combined NCSCST). What was the core ruling?\n1. The Commission has the power to issue temporary injunctions (stay orders) against government transfers/promotions.\n2. The Commission's 'civil court powers' are strictly limited to summoning witnesses and producing documents for investigation.\n3. The Commission cannot adjudicate on the rights of the parties or issue binding legal decrees.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The SC specifically struck down the Commission's assumption that it could issue interim injunctions or stay orders preventing executive actions (like transfers). It clarified that while it has procedural powers of a civil court to gather evidence (Statement 2), it lacks substantive adjudicatory powers to declare binding decrees (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Explanatory Memorandum' accompanying the NCSC report (Article 338(6)):\n1. It details the action taken or proposed to be taken on the recommendations relating to the Union.\n2. It must provide reasons for the non-acceptance, if any, of any of such recommendations.\n3. Only the Prime Minister can draft and table this memorandum.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The Memorandum is drafted by the concerned nodal Ministry (Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment) on behalf of the Government and tabulated procedurally; it does not explicitly require the PM's personal drafting. Statements 1 and 2 mirror the constitutional text ensuring transparency.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"The NCSC's mandate investigates not just constitutional safeguards but also those under:\n1. Laws made by Parliament specifically for SCs (like PoA Act).\n2. Any order of the Government for the time being in force regarding SCs.\n3. International human rights treaties explicitly overriding domestic laws.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; India follows a dualist system where treaties don't override domestic law automatically. The NCSC focuses on domestic constitutional and statutory safeguards. Article 338(5)(a) explicitly mandates the NCSC to investigate safeguards under the Constitution, under any Law, or under any Government order (thus covering Acts like the SC/ST PoA Act and executive orders regarding reservation rosters).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"If the NCSC receives a complaint regarding the breach of reservation rosters in a Central Public Sector Enterprise (CPSE):\n1. It can summon the Chairman/MD of the CPSE to produce the roster registers.\n2. It can annul the appointments incorrectly made against the SC quota.\n3. It can submit its findings/recommendations to the administrative ministry commanding them to rectify the breach.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The NCSC cannot legally 'annul' or cancel appointments; that requires a court order or executive rectification. It can, however, summon the MD (Statement 1) and recommend the ministry/management to rectify the breach (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the rules determining the NCSC composition:\n1. The Constitution stipulates that at least one member must be a woman.\n2. The Constitution mandates that all members must themselves belong to the Scheduled Castes.\n3. The President's Rules (2004) mandate that at least one member shall be a woman.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"Statements 1 and 2 are incorrect because the *text* of the Constitution (Article 338) does NOT specify gender or caste requirements for the members. It leaves the conditions to be determined by the President by rule. Under the *President's rules* (2004), it is prescribed that at least one member must be a woman, and conventionally members are drawn from SC backgrounds. Only 3 is correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the procedure followed by the NCSC in discharging its functions:\n1. It is bound by the strict procedural dictates of the Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC/BNSS).\n2. The Commission has the power to regulate its own procedure.\n3. It can initiate suo-motu inquiries without waiting for a formal written complaint.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Article 338(4) explicitly guarantees that 'The Commission shall have the power to regulate its own procedure'. While it enjoys certain Civil Court powers under CPC for summoning, it is not bound by CrPC. Statement 3 is correct; its investigatory mandate allows suo-motu cognizance.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Which of the following scenarios falls OUTSIDE the jurisdiction of the NCSC?\n1. A dispute over whether the 'Banjara' community should be added to the SC list instead of the OBC list.\n2. A complaint regarding the denial of promotion to an SC employee in a private sector software company without government ties.\n3. A complaint of atrocities against an SC family by upper-caste members in a village.\nSelect the correct answer:","options":["1 and 2 only","2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is correct (outside purview) because constitutional reservations and NCSC safeguards apply to employment under the State (Article 12), not pure private entities without state aid. Scenarios 1 and 3 are within its advisory/investigatory purview.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Can the NCSC evaluate the implementation of the SC Sub-Plan (now Scheduled Caste Sub-Area Plan/Allocation)?\n1. Yes, evaluating the socio-economic development plan falls under Article 338(5)(c).\n2. No, financial allocations are strictly the domain of the C&AG and Finance Commission.\n3. Yes, it frequently reviews state budgets to ensure proportional fund allocation for SC development.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The NCSC is specifically mandated to participate in and advise on the 'planning process of socio-economic development' of the SCs. Therefore, it routinely reviews and questions governments on the proper utilization and allocation of funds under the SC Sub-Plan (Statements 1 and 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional position of Anglo-Indians:\n1. The 104th Amendment Act, 2019, extended the reservation for Anglo-Indians in the Lok Sabha.\n2. Despite changes to their legislative reservation, the NCSC continues to investigate their constitutional safeguards under Article 338(10).\n3. Before 2004, their safeguards were monitored by the National Commission for Minorities exclusively.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; the 104th Amendment *ceased* the reservation for Anglo-Indians in legislatures (it did not extend it beyond 2020). Statement 3 is incorrect; Article 338 always covered Anglo-Indians. Statement 2 is correct; their other cultural/educational safeguards (e.g., grants) are still under the NCSC purview.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between the NCSC and the Ministry of Social Justice & Empowerment (MSJE):\n1. MSJE is the executive branch responsible for implementing schemes, while NCSC is the independent constitutional watchdog.\n2. NCSC's budget is determined entirely by MSJE and cannot be debated in Parliament.\n3. The Chairperson of NCSC reports directly to the MSJE Secretary.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; while routed via MSJE, the budget is passed by Parliament. Statement 3 is drastically incorrect; the Chairperson ranks as a Cabinet Minister and submits reports directly to the President, not a Secretary. Statement 1 perfectly captures their complementary but distinct roles.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"If a victim from an SC community files an identical complaint with the NCSC, the NHRC (National Human Rights Commission), and the High Court simultaneously:\n1. The NCSC must immediately halt its inquiry because the matter is sub-judice in the High Court.\n2. The NCSC can continue parallel investigations regardless of court proceedings.\n3. By convention/rules, constitutional/statutory commissions often decline or pause inquiries into matters that are actively sub-judice before a constitutional court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"While not an absolute constitutional bar, under their own Rules of Procedure and general legal prudence, Commissions (NCSC, NHRC, etc.) generally decline to take up or pause inquiries if the exact matter is actively sub-judice before a High Court or the Supreme Court, to avoid judicial conflict. (Hence Statement 2 is generally practically false; Statement 1 is too absolute, it's a procedural choice, making 3 the most accurate).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'consultation' required under Article 338(9):\nIf a State Government decides to alter the reservation roster points for SC candidates in direct recruitment:\n1. It must consult the NCSC before finalizing the policy.\n2. If the NCSC advises against it, the State cannot proceed under any circumstance.\n3. Seeking advice validates the procedural requirement, even if the state decides overriding political reasons demand bypassing the advice.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The advice is not a binding veto. Consultation must be substantive and the views considered, but the final executive/legislative decision remains with the State (which will be politically accountable and legally challengeable if arbitrary). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the NCSC's state offices:\n1. State offices function under the administrative control of the State Chief Minister.\n2. They assist the NCSC headquarters in monitoring the implementation of safeguards at the grassroots level.\n3. They have the power to independently table reports in the State Legislature bypassing the headquarters.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; they function under the administrative control of the NCSC headquarters (Union). Statement 3 is incorrect; all reports must be finalized by the core Commission and submitted via the President/Governor mechanism. Statement 2 is correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Under what condition is the Chairperson of the NCSC usually removed?\n1. By impeachment via a special majority in Parliament.\n2. By the President on grounds of misbehaviour or incapacity, often tied to their Service Rules or pleasure of the President.\n3. Automatically when the ruling party at the Centre changes.\nWhich is correct?","options":["2 only","1 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"The NCSC Chairperson serves at the pleasure of the President as guided by the Tenure Rules, 2004. Unlike Judges or the Election Commissioner, there is no convoluted Parliamentary impeachment process (Statement 1 is false). While political changes often lead to resignations, it's not a constitutional 'automatic' removal (Statement 3 is false).","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the NCSC's role in the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act:\n1. The Act explicitly makes the NCSC the sole appellate court against trial court sentences.\n2. The NCSC monitors the implementation of the Act, such as checking conviction rates and the payment of compensation to victims.\n3. The NCSC cannot direct the police to register an FIR if they have refused to do so.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is blatantly incorrect (it's not a court). Statement 3 is incorrect; while it can't issue a judicial 'mandamus', it frequently summons the DGP/SP and issues strong 'recommendations/directions' that effectively force the registration of FIRs where police dragged their feet. Statement 2 accurately defines its monitoring role.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},
    {"text":"What is the effect of the NCSC being a Constitutional body rather than a Statutory one (like the NHRC)?\n1. Its existence cannot be abolished by a simple Act of Parliament.\n2. Its functions and core powers (like civil court status) are immunized from arbitrary alteration by simple majority.\n3. The government can dissolve it via an executive order during a National Emergency.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. A constitutional body cannot be dissolved by executive order, even during an Emergency, without following the constitutional amendment/suspension procedures. Statements 1 and 2 correctly outline the deep entrenchment and protection a Constitutional status provides.","chapter_number":53,"level":2},

    # CH53 National Commission for SCs L3 (+29)
    {"text":"Consider the historical necessity that led to the 65th Amendment Act (1990) upgrading the Special Officer to a multi-member Commission:\n1. Reports of the single Special Officer were often ignored and seen as lacking the institutional weight of a multi-member body.\n2. The executive felt that a multi-member commission would provide diverse representation and broader perspective.\n3. The Supreme Court mandated the creation of a multi-member body via a public interest litigation (PIL).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is historically incorrect. The shift was driven by political demands, civil society pressure, and administrative reform (reflected in parliamentary debates), not a Supreme Court mandate. Statements 1 and 2 accurately reflect the rationale for the 65th Amendment.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze Supreme Court precedents limiting the adjudicatory powers of the NCSC (e.g., *Collector, Bilaspur v. Ajit P.K. Jogi*, 2011):\n1. The Court ruled that NCSC cannot usurp the jurisdiction of an Election Tribunal or civil court regarding caste certificate validity.\n2. The NCSC cannot issue binding injunctions directing authorities to issue a caste certificate.\n3. The civil court powers granted under Article 338(8) are distinct from substantive powers to grant relief.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. In *Ajit P.K. Jogi*, the SC held that NCSC had exceeded its jurisdiction by 'decreeing' on the caste status of a person (which impacts election eligibility). It clarified that Art 338(8) grants procedural powers to aid investigation, not substantive adjudicatory powers to grant final legal relief.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Examine the mandate \"to participate and advise on the planning process of socio-economic development of the Scheduled Castes\" [Article 338(5)(c)]:\n1. This means the NCSC is an ex-officio member of the NITI Aayog Governing Council.\n2. The NCSC reviews the implementation of the Scheduled Caste Sub-Area Plan (SCSP) ensuring funds are proportional to the SC population (approx 16.6%).\n3. The NCSC can legally block the Union Budget if the SCSP allocation targets are unmet.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. NCSC is not an ex-officio member of NITI Aayog (though it interacts with them). Statement 3 is incorrect. NCSC is an advisory body and has no veto over the Parliamentary Budget process. Statement 2 is true in practice; NCSC reviews allocations under the SCSP to ensure demographic proportionality.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the interaction between Article 338 (NCSC) and Article 341 (Scheduled Castes Order):\n1. The NCSC is continuously consulted regarding proposals from states to add or delete communities from the SC list.\n2. The NCSC independently verifies ethnological data before advising the Government on these additions/deletions.\n3. If the NCSC rejects a state's proposal, Parliament lacks the legislative competence to alter the SC list for that state.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Under Article 341(2), Parliament has absolute sovereign competence to modify the SC list; the NCSC's advice, while highly crucial and procedurally mandatory under Ministry guidelines, doesn't legally strip Parliament of its legislative power. Statements 1 and 2 are correct reflections of the process followed before introducing modifying bills.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"What is the legal standing of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes Rules, 2004 (Conditions of Service) regarding tenure?\n1. Members have a fixed tenure of exactly 65 years of age or 5 years, similar to UPSC members.\n2. Members serve for three years, eligible for reappointment for only one additional term.\n3. These rules were unilaterally drafted by the NCSC itself under its power to regulate procedure.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; the term is 3 years. Statement 3 is incorrect; the rules detailing conditions of service are drafted by the Ministry of Social Justice and approved by the President under Article 338(2), not by the Commission itself (they regulate their *operational procedure* under 338(4), not their service rules). Statement 2 correctly reflects the 3-year term and 2-term limit.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"A major PSU ignores the reservation roster points, carrying forward unreserved vacancies without NCSC consultation. If the NCSC takes suo-motu cognizance:\n1. The NCSC can summon the HR Director of the PSU under its civil court powers.\n2. If the HR Director ignores the summons, the NCSC can issue a bailable/non-bailable warrant for their appearance.\n3. The NCSC can levy heavy financial penalties on the PSU for violating the roster.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. As repeatedly emphasized by courts, the NCSC cannot levy penalties or issue substantive decrees; it recommends action (like urging the Ministry to initiate disciplinary proceedings). Statements 1 and 2 are true; under civil court powers (CPC), a commission can issue summons and use warrants to compel attendance if ignored.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the concept of \"Consultation\" under Article 338(9) vs Article 320(3) (SPSC consultation):\n1. Constitutional courts have typically interpreted both as \"directory\" rather than \"mandatory\" for validating the final executive order.\n2. However, failure to consult NCSC on a major policy affecting SCs faces stricter judicial scrutiny regarding violations of Article 14 and 15(4).\n3. Rejection of SPSC advice must be explained to the legislature, while rejection of NCSC advice on a policy matter requires no such explanation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is blatantly incorrect. Article 338(6) & (7) stringently require that if NCSC's recommendations in its reports are rejected, the Government must lay an explanatory memorandum before Parliament/State Legislature. Statements 1 and 2 capture the nuanced legal reality: while technically directory (not automatically voiding the policy), failure to consult bodies like NCSC risks the policy being struck down as arbitrary/unconstitutional.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between the 'Investigatory' (Art 338(5)(a)) and 'Inquiry' (Art 338(5)(b)) functions of NCSC:\n1. Investigation is broader, covering the general functioning of safeguards (e.g., analyzing nationwide untouchability trends).\n2. Inquiry is narrower, focusing on specific complaints relating to deprivation of rights of individuals or groups.\n3. The civil court powers (summoning, evidence on oath) only apply to Inquiries, not Investigations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Article 338(8) states: \"The Commission shall, while investigating any matter referred to in sub-clause (a) or inquiring into any complaint referred to in sub-clause (b) of clause (5), have all the powers of a civil court.\" Thus, civil court powers apply to both. Statements 1 and 2 correctly differentiate the conceptual scopes.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the NCSC's interaction with the National Human Rights Commission (NHRC):\n1. The Chairperson of the NCSC is an ex-officio member of the NHRC.\n2. This statutory arrangement ensures coordination on human rights issues involving vulnerable communities.\n3. The NCSC must seek prior permission from the NHRC before investigating any custodial death of an SC individual.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is totally incorrect. The NCSC is an independent Constitutional body and does not need permission from the NHRC (a statutory body) for its investigations. However, under the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993, the Chairperson of NCSC is indeed an ex-officio member of the NHRC (Statement 1) to ensure structural coordination (Statement 2).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Examine the implications of the 102nd Amendment (2018) on the NCSC:\n1. It stripped the NCSC of its mandate to investigate the safeguards for the Socially and Educationally Backward Classes (SEBCs/OBCs).\n2. It explicitly transferred the power to notify OBCs from the NCSC to the Supreme Court.\n3. It established the National Commission for Backward Classes (Art 338B) as a parallel body mirroring the design of Article 338.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The power to notify SEBCs was not given to the Supreme Court; it was vested in the President (under the new Article 342A), similar to SCs (Art 341). Statements 1 and 3 are correct representations of the 102nd Amendment that removed OBCs from NCSC’s purview and created the NCBC (Art 338B).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the structural constraints often cited by Parliamentary Committees regarding the NCSC's effectiveness:\n1. The Commission is heavily dependent on the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment for infrastructure, budget, and manpower.\n2. Unlike the Election Commission, the NCSC lacks independent budgetary control (it is not charged on the Consolidated Fund).\n3. The high percentage of delays in tabling the Action Taken Reports by State Governments blunts the impact of its recommendations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements identify recognized structural constraints. NCSC's finances are voted (not charged), and it functionally relies on the MSJE for Secretariat staff (Statements 1 and 2). Non-compliance and immense delays by states in tabling reports/ATRs is a frequent complaint logged by the Commission itself (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"A State Government launches a land-redistribution policy focusing entirely on general category poor, completely bypassing the SC demographic, and does not consult the NCSC. The NCSC takes cognizance:\n1. It can issue a writ of mandamus compelling the State to allocate land to SCs.\n2. It can produce an investigative report highlighting the breach of Article 46 and lack of Article 338(9) consultation.\n3. If tabled in the Assembly, the State Government must formally respond to the NCSC's objections via the Action Taken Memorandum.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; NCSC does not possess writ jurisdiction (Articles 32/226 belong to SC/HC). However, its investigatory and reporting mechanism is powerful: it can highlight constitutional breaches (Statement 2) and force a political/legislative response via the mandatory Action Taken Memorandum (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional position of the Anglo-Indian community vis-à-vis the NCSC:\n1. Anglo-Indians are defined under Article 366(2).\n2. Historically, they were included under Article 338(10) because their constitutional safeguards (like Articles 336 and 337 regarding services and education) needed monitoring.\n3. The 104th Amendment completely removed the phrase 'Anglo-Indian' from Article 338(10).\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The 104th Amendment ceased the legislative reservation (Art 334), but it did NOT amend Article 338(10). The NCSC theoretically still holds the monitoring mandate for Anglo-Indians, though the *specific* safeguards (like educational grants) have lapsed by their own constitutional timelines. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the NCSC's powers relating to the 'Discovery and Production of any Document' (Art 338(8)(b)):\n1. The NCSC can override the Official Secrets Act and demand highly classified defense documents unrelated to SC safeguards.\n2. If a District Magistrate claims a document is 'privileged state communication' under the Evidence Act, the NCSC has the competence of a civil court to evaluate that claim.\n3. Failure to produce a demanded document can be prosecuted under the Indian Penal Code (now BNS) for disobeying a public servant.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; its powers are restricted to matters 'while investigating any matter referred to in sub-clause (a)...'. Therefore, asking for unrelated defense documents is ultra vires. However, within its purview, it evaluates claims of privilege just like a civil court (Statement 2), and non-compliance with its summons can trigger perjury/disobedience clauses under the IPC/BNS (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the phrase 'all matters relating to the safeguards provided' under Article 338(5)(a):\n1. This includes reservations under Article 16(4).\n2. It includes protection against untouchability under Article 17.\n3. It includes examining the adequacy of representation under Article 335.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The \"safeguards\" encompass fundamental rights against discrimination (Art 15, 17), service safeguards (Art 16(4), 335), and political safeguards (Art 330, 332). The NCSC oversees the enforcement of this entire constitutional spectrum.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the NCSC's intervention in higher education (e.g., University Grants Commission / central universities):\n1. It frequently investigates complaints of discrimination in student admissions and hostel allocations.\n2. It monitors the strict implementation of reservation rosters in faculty recruitment under the Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Teachers' Cadre) Act, 2019.\n3. It has the Constitutional power to dismiss a Vice-Chancellor if found guilty of caste discrimination.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The NCSC cannot dismiss authorities directly (it lacks executive/penal decree power). It recommends such actions to the appointing authority (the President/Visitor or the Chancellor). Statements 1 and 2 represent core activities of the NCSC in protecting SC safeguards in academia.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"What is the legal implication if the NCSC issues a 'sweeping direction' (e.g., directing a state to ban a particular textbook it finds derogatory to SCs)?\n1. It must be immediately obeyed by the State Government under threat of constitutional breakdown (Article 356).\n2. The Constitution does not grant the NCSC the power to issue such executive/legislative bans.\n3. The aggrieved publisher can challenge the NCSC's direction in a High Court under Article 226.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The NCSC cannot issue binding mandates or bans on its own; it can only recommend the government do so. Therefore, if it attempts to issue a binding 'order', it acts ultra vires (Statement 2). An aggrieved party can easily challenge this overreach in a High Court (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Special Officer' under the original Article 338:\n1. The Special Officer was tasked with investigating safeguards for SCs, STs, and Anglo-Indians.\n2. Despite the creation of an executive multi-member commission in 1978, the Special Officer continued to exist because a Constitutional provision cannot be overridden by an executive resolution.\n3. The Special Officer was finally abolished by the 89th Amendment Act.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The Special Officer was abolished and replaced by the multi-member constitutional Commission by the *65th Amendment Act, 1990*, not the 89th (which bifurcated the SC/ST commission). Statements 1 and 2 correctly outline the historical trajectory of the office.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Examine the NCSC's role in the context of the Atrocities Act (PoA Act, 1989):\n1. It can direct the state government to provide immediate monetary relief to the victims according to the PoA rules.\n2. It can evaluate whether Special Courts and Exclusive Special Courts have been established in districts as mandated by the Act.\n3. It takes over the prosecution of the accused in the Special Courts from the public prosecutor.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Prosecuting a criminal case is the job of the State (via the Public Prosecutor) in front of the judiciary. The NCSC cannot usurp the role of the state prosecutor. Statements 1 and 2 are true; it actively monitors and issues recommendations (directives) ensuring the executive fulfills its statutory duties under the PoA Rules regarding relief and infrastructure.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the requirement that reports are laid before Parliament (Art 338(6)):\n1. Delays in tabling the report do not invalidate the report itself.\n2. If the Government disagrees with a recommendation, it must disclose the specific recommendation and the reason for non-acceptance.\n3. The President can redact parts of the report citing national security before placing it in Parliament.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Article 338(6) states the President shall cause \"all such reports to be laid\". There is no constitutional redaction clause allowing the executive to censor the independent Commission's report before Parliament sees it. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect the systemic realities and the transparency requirement.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"What happens if an NCSC member acts with demonstrable partisan bias or violates the code of conduct?\n1. The President has the power to remove them under the tenure rules governing their service.\n2. The Supreme Court must first conduct a mandatory inquiry similar to the SPSC/UPSC removal process.\n3. They have absolute immunity from removal during their 3-year term.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 and 3 are incorrect. Unlike Article 317 (which mandates an SC inquiry for UPSC/SPSC members), Article 338 leaves the conditions of service to be determined by the President. The NCSC Rules (2004) allow the President to remove the Chairperson or any Member on grounds like insolvency, moral turpitude, or acting in a manner detrimental to the public interest. The rigid constitutional protection of an SC inquiry does not apply here.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the NCSC's role when a state enacts a law declaring a private sector reservation mandate (e.g., Haryana's local quota law):\n1. As private sector reservations (if targeting SCs) represent a major policy matter, the state MUST consult the NCSC under Article 338(9).\n2. The NCSC can strike down the state law if it violates the basic structure of the Constitution.\n3. The NCSC's investigation would focus on whether the law effectively advances the safeguards of the Scheduled Castes.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect because the NCSC does not possess judicial review powers to 'strike down' a law; only Constitutional Courts do. Statements 1 and 3 are correct. Enacting reservation laws explicitly engages the consultation requirement under Article 338(9), and it would be evaluated based on its efficacy for SCs.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyze the 'Receiving Evidence on Affidavits' power (Art 338(8)(c)):\n1. It allows the NCSC to gather sworn written testimonies without requiring physical presence.\n2. Lying on such an affidavit submitted to the NCSC constitutes the crime of perjury.\n3. It gives the NCSC the power to order wire-taps to verify the affidavit.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is radically incorrect. The power to intercept communications (wire-taps) is a severe state police power under the Telegraph Act/IT Act, subject to strict Home Ministry oversight. A civil court (and by extension, the NCSC) does not possess it. Statements 1 and 2 are standard features of Indian evidence law regarding affidavits submitted to empowered fact-finding bodies.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"If an individual files a complaint to the NCSC asking for a financial grant for establishing a startup:\n1. The NCSC can investigate if the individual was unfairly denied a loan under specific SC government schemes.\n2. The NCSC can sanction the loan directly from its own budget.\n3. The NCSC is barred from advising banks to follow priority sector lending norms for SCs.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","3 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect (NCSC is not a funding/grant body). Statement 3 is incorrect (advising on socio-economic planning includes banking and credit access). Statement 1 is correct; inquiring into specific deprivations of rights (like discrimination in a government loan scheme) is its core mandate.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"How does the NCSC approach the \"Creamy Layer\" debate regarding Scheduled Castes?\n1. Historically, the NCSC has usually opposed applying the creamy layer concept to SC reservation.\n2. The Constitution explicitly instructs the NCSC to identify and exclude the creamy layer among SCs annually.\n3. If the Supreme Court introduces a creamy layer concept for SCs, the NCSC is bound to monitor its implementation within government services.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The Constitution does NOT mention 'creamy layer' for SCs, it arose via judicial interpretation for OBCs and debates for SCs. Statement 1 is factually true—NCSC has frequently argued against it for SCs, citing the persistence of untouchability regardless of economic status. Statement 3 is true; if the apex court establishes it as the law of the land, the NCSC must monitor safeguards under that prevailing legal framework.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the NCSC's relationship directly with Parliament:\n1. The Chairperson has the right to speak and participate in the proceedings of Lok Sabha without voting.\n2. Parliament can form a Committee on the Welfare of SCs and STs, to which the NCSC reports are often referred.\n3. A constitutional amendment would be required to merge the NCSC and NCST back into one body.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The right to speak in proceedings belongs to Ministers and the Attorney General (Art 88), not structural Commission chairpersons. Statement 2 is correct (the Parliamentary Committee on SC/ST Welfare exists and scrutinizes these reports). Statement 3 is correct (undoing the 89th Amendment requires another Constitutional Amendment).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional obligation of the Union under Article 338(9):\n1. The term 'consult' implies that the government must table the NCSC's views on a policy in the public domain before passing a law.\n2. The Supreme Court has clarified that consultation requires the government to supply the NCSC with all relevant facts and materials before taking a decision.\n3. State Governors are exempt from this consultation requirement when passing Ordinances.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; while transparency is ideal, the Constitution does not mandate 'tabling views in public domain' prior to legislation—only consultation with the body. Statement 3 is incorrect; the \"State Government\" (which includes the Governor acting on advice or via Ordinance) must consult. Statement 2 correctly captures the judicial interpretation of 'meaningful consultation'.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the NCSC's mandate regarding \"Untouchability\" (Article 17):\n1. It investigates the enforcement of the Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955.\n2. It can sentence an offender to a maximum of 6 months imprisonment if found guilty of untouchability practices.\n3. It evaluates the awareness and educational campaigns run by states to eradicate untouchability.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is again incorrect. The NCSC has no penal/sentencing powers. Courts (Judicial Magistrates) enforce sentences under the PCR Act. The NCSC monitors and investigates the *efficacy* of the system (investigating police inaction, reviewing awareness campaigns), making Statements 1 and 3 correct.","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider a scenario where the NCSC summons a Chief Secretary, who refuses to appear:\n1. The NCSC can move the High Court seeking the initiation of contempt of court proceedings against the officer.\n2. The NCSC can use police force to physically arrest and bring the officer to its headquarters.\n3. The refusal diminishes the NCSC's authority unless the Central Government enforces the NCSC's directive.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. While it has civil court powers to issue warrants, deploying police to physically arrest a top state bureaucrat directly stretches beyond its functional framework without court/executive backing. In reality, defying summons invites legal trouble via High Court contempt references or IPC/BNS prosecution (Statement 1) and severely hits authority unless the nodal executive reinforces it (Statement 3).","chapter_number":53,"level":3},
]
